<?php

namespace Illuminate\Support\Facades;

/**
 * The facade root `Http\Client\Factory` forwards every method it does not declare (through `__call`)
 * to a fresh, sync `PendingRequest`. The `@mixin` makes Psalm analyse `Http::get($url)` as that
 * PendingRequest instance call: a static call consults a mixin target's real method before the
 * facade's generated `@method` tags (decisions.md, the `Schema` facade). So the facade shares the
 * PendingRequest stub's return types, params and `ssrf` sinks, `Http::async()` narrows to
 * AsyncPendingRequest, and a verb added to the PendingRequest stub needs no edit here.
 *
 * Real methods outrank the mixin. Factory's own methods win at runtime over `__call`, so the one
 * whose PendingRequest namesake has a different signature is restated with the facade tag below.
 * The Macroable statics (`macro`, `mixin`, `hasMacro`, `flushMacros`) share one signature on both.
 *
 * Caveats: the mixin must stay non-generic (`@mixin PendingRequest<bool>` makes Psalm resolve `$this` to
 * the facade). Psalm forwards on a receiver without template arguments, so a fluent result is
 * `PendingRequest&static` and a typed callback of `tap(Http::withHeaders(...), ...)` reports a false
 * ArgumentTypeCoercion (HttpFacadeTapKnownLimitation.phpt).
 *
 * The stub docblock replaces the sealing flag Laravel's `@method` tags set on the vendor class, so the
 * seal is restated to keep a misspelled call (`Http::psot()`) reported.
 *
 * @mixin \Illuminate\Http\Client\PendingRequest
 * @psalm-seal-methods
 */
class Http extends Facade
{
    /**
     * Allow stray, unfaked requests entirely, or optionally allow only specific URLs.
     *
     * Factory's method: PendingRequest's namesake requires `$only` and returns the pending request.
     *
     * @param  array|null  $only
     * @return \Illuminate\Http\Client\Factory
     */
    public static function allowStrayRequests($only = null) {}
}
